Cocktail onion:
{{refimprove|date=May 2023}}
{{Short description|Pearl onion pickled in a brine}}
{{Infobox drink
| name        = Cocktail onion
| image       = File:Cocktail onions.JPG  
| caption     = A jar of cocktail onions. 
| type        = [[Cocktail garnish]]}}

A '''cocktail onion''' is usually a [[pearl onion]] pickled in a [[brine]] with small amounts of [[turmeric]] and [[paprika]].<ref name="smith" /> Pearl onions are usually chosen for their natural sweetness, but other sweet onions such as the crystal wax, also known as the white Bermuda, are also sometimes used. In many cases, white varieties of these sweet onions are used, since many consumers expect cocktail onions to be white. However, yellow or red sweet onions may be used as well. In northern [[California cuisine]] some [[Haute cuisine|haute]] bars may use sliced [[red onion]] [[pickled]] in [[vinegar]]. Some recipes also call for the onions to be packed in white [[vermouth]] instead.<ref name="smith" />

Generally, the onion retains a slightly crunchy texture through the brining process, which can add a different mouthfeel to the drinking experience. Since the cocktail onion is made from a sweet onion, it is unlikely to upset the digestion with a sulfurous or eye-watering taste, although some cultures use more pungent onions as cocktail garnishes.

==Use as a garnish==
[[File:Gibson cocktail.tif|thumb|Gibson cocktail using green cocktail onion|left]]While not as widely used as more common garnishes such as [[Olive (fruit)|olives]] or lemon twists, the cocktail onion is the signature garnish of the [[Gibson (cocktail)|Gibson]], which consists of a standard [[Martini (cocktail)|Martini]] garnished with a cocktail onion instead of the standard olive.<ref name="smith">{{cite book |last1=Smith |first1=David T. |title=The Gin Dictionary |date=5 April 2018 |publisher=Octopus |isbn=9781784724894 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=SIlBDwAAQBAJ |access-date=3 May 2023}}</ref>

