Gatorwine:
{{Short description|Viral cocktail made up of red wine and Gatorade}}
{{Sources exist|date=August 2025}}
{{Infobox drink
| name        = Gatorwine   
| type        = [[Wine cocktail]]
| base        = 
}}

'''Gatorwine''' is a [[Viral phenomenon|viral]] [[cocktail]] made up of [[red wine]] and [[Gatorade]]. The original recipe specifies using equal parts light blue (either Glacier Freeze or Cool Blue) gatorade and [[red wine]] "cheap, under 12 dollars", served from a chilled glass or on the rocks.

Created by user GoatsNowhere, it was submitted to the [[YouTube]] channel [[Binging with Babish]] for the video titled ''Ranking Your STRANGEST Recipes | Best with Babish'' on Jul 30, 2024 where it was made with one part Gatorade Zero Cool Blue and a [[E & J Gallo Winery|Barefoot]] [[pinot noir]]. The host [[Andrew Rea]] quipped about its "versatility" and addition of [[electrolytes]], noting it was similar to a [[sangria]] and in his words “In a very disgusting, perverse way, I like it.”. He continued to remake and drink the cocktail throughout the episode, often stopping to sing [[Parody music|song parodies]] and opine how the gatorwine helped him get through some of the less palatable recipes. The video quickly received over 3 million views and soon became [[meme]]tic throughout the Babish fandom before trending on [[TikTok]] and [[Reddit]], eventually making it to various news outlets. Rea would later follow-up with a dedicated gatorwine video.<ref>{{cite web | title=Ranking Your STRANGEST Recipes &#124; Best with Babish | website=[[YouTube]] | date=30 July 2024 | url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5inVS8RSz7Q }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=Ranking 24 Flavors of Gatorade (An Excuse To Make Gatorwine) &#124; Best with Babish | website=[[YouTube]] | date= Sep 28, 2024 | url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MfZ9kgXfDxE&t=331s }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=Consider: Gatorwine &#124; Defector | date=August 27, 2024 | url=https://defector.com/consider-gatorwine }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=Hydration With a Buzz: Introducing The Newest Viral Drink, Gatorwine | date=August 20, 2024 | url=https://klaw.com/what-is-gatorwine/ |publisher=KLAW 101}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=What Is Gatorwine? TikTok's Latest Drink Invention To Stretch Cheap Wine | date=August 26, 2024 | url=https://www.yahoo.com/lifestyle/gatorwine-tiktoks-latest-drink-invention-214000651.html?guccounter=1&guce_referrer=aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZ29vZ2xlLmNvbS8&guce_referrer_sig=AQAAADSAmx7TdnjiX3rMGy8bI6h4wcv9rrqjMcyb3yjMwcnMG8pOt97srdEES_AUIuYm3kbyvpcVZ5Yblk54O_SRqjiqyYLe3w46c4N_c1w_tewS_6hFGRicxVs2VBx_rRbVQA5BodBRNN-flDt3KLkOtpKiyW8DPvG2XGyd9Bkc8HgH |publisher=Yahoo!Life}}</ref>

Popular variations on the cocktail include mixing different varieties of gatorade and wine. Mixologist content-creator Prescott Vanmeyer III suggested the idea could be used to stretch one's red wine supply, insisting that "Glacier Freeze" was the optimal pairing. The sports drink's sugar and sodium is noted to balance out acidity and bitterness at the cost of complexity, but may make wine more approachable for those not used to drinking it. Speculation that the addition of Gatorade may reduce the risk of [[hangover]] however is perhaps overexaggerated. Phoebe Miles, daughter of [[Robert Cade|James Cade]], the creator of Gatorade; said he would have gotten a laugh from the mixture.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.orlandosentinel.com/2024/10/19/gatorwine-viral-smash-mixes-sports-drink-with-fruit-of-the-vine/ |title='Gatorwine' viral smash mixes sports drink with fruit of the vine |date=19 October 2024 |publisher=Orlando Sentinel}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=Gatorwine, the viral Gatorade and wine combo, is here to ruin your dinner party | date=29 August 2024 | url=https://www.today.com/food/trends/gatorwine-rcna168796 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.pastemagazine.com/drink/gatorwine/gatorwine-millennial-college |title=Gatorwine Is A Millennial College Fever Dream |date=September 4, 2024 |publisher=Paste}}</ref>

