May wine:
{{Short description|German aromatized wine served in spring}}
{{For|the Broadway musical|May Wine}}
{{Refimprove|date=August 2009}}
[[File:Maibowle in Dresden.jpg|thumb|May wine with strawberries and a sprig of woodruff]]
'''May wine''', also known as '''Maitrank''', '''Maiwein''', '''Maibowle''' and '''Waldmeisterbowle''', is an [[aromatized wine]] popular in [[Germany]], sometimes made into a punch. May wine is served in the spring, traditionally on the [[May Day]] holiday. The base is made by steeping the fragrant creeping [[herb]] [[Galium odoratum|sweet woodruff]] (''Galium odoratum'', sometimes called ''Asperula odorata'', known in Germany as ''Waldmeister'') that grows in the forests of [[Northern Europe]] in a white [[German wine]]. It is the specialty of the town of [[Arlon]], in the south of [[Belgium]].

==Ingredients==
[[Image:Waldmeister.jpg|thumb|upright|''[[Galium odoratum]]'' (woodruff) is a principal ingredient in the base of May wine]]

The [[Council of the European Communities]] legally defines Maiwein as:
{{quote|[A]n aromatized drink obtained from wine with added ''Asperula odorata'' [Galium odoratum] plants or extracts thereof so as to ensure a predominant taste of ''Asperula odorata''.<ref>[http://www.wipo.int/wipolex/en/text.jsp?file_id=126932 EEC Regulation on "Aromatized Wines" (No. 1601/91/EEC)]</ref>}}

To make a [[Punch (drink)|punch]] (''Maibowle'', May punch) ingredients such as [[brandy]], [[sparkling wine]] (or [[carbonated water]]) and [[sugar]] may also be added to Maywein.{{citation needed|date=June 2022}}

Since [[strawberries]] are in season at that time of year, they are often floated in the drink. 

==Production==
A variation might include strawberry flavoring or fermentation techniques to make it lightly sparkling.{{Citation needed|date=August 2009}} Commercial May wine produced in Germany has been made with artificial flavoring and coloring agents since the 1980s.{{citation needed|date=June 2022}}

Maywein is produced in German cultural regions of the [[United States]] such as [[Frankenmuth, Michigan]].{{cn|date=February 2024}}

