Oleo saccharum:
{{Short description|Sugar-oil mixture}}
'''Oleo saccharum''' ("oil sugar") is a [[White sugar|sugar]]-[[Essential oil|oil]] mixture produced by coating [[citrus]] or other oil-rich [[Peel (fruit)|fruit rinds]] in an excess of sugar. The essential oils [[Extraction (chemistry)|extracted]] into the sugar give a concentrated [[Aroma compound|aromatic]] mixture rich in [[terpene]]s. Because the oils are [[hydrophobic]] and [[Volatility (chemistry)|volatile]], they cannot be obtained through simple [[Aqueous solution|aqueous]] extraction processes. In [[Bartender|mixology]], oleo saccharum can be used to sweeten [[Drink|beverages]] by their direct use or as an ingredient in flavored [[Syrup|syrups]]. Oleo saccharum is a key component in many [[Punch (drink)|punch]] recipes, being listed as an ingredient as early as 1670.<ref name="Caiafa 2015">{{cite book|last=Caiafa|first=F.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=erYBDAAAQBAJ&pg=PA358|title=The Waldorf Astoria Bar Book|publisher=Penguin Publishing Group|year=2015|isbn=978-0-14-312480-1|pages=358–359}}</ref><ref name="McCammonWondrich2016">{{cite book|author1=Ross McCammon|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=JDMBCwAAQBAJ&pg=PA22|title=Drink Like a Man: The Only Cocktail Guide Anyone Really Needs|author2=David Wondrich|date=7 June 2016|publisher=Chronicle Books LLC|isbn=978-1-4521-4355-2|pages=22–}}</ref><ref name="Morgenthaler2014">{{cite book|author=Jeffrey Morgenthaler|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=OZkWAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA108|title=The Bar Book: Elements of Cocktail Technique|date=3 June 2014|publisher=Chronicle Books LLC|isbn=978-1-4521-3027-9|pages=108–}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Oleo Saccharum|url=https://www.diffordsguide.com/encyclopedia/234/cocktails/oleo-saccharum|access-date=2020-08-20|website=www.diffordsguide.com|language=en}}</ref> 

Oil extraction is greatly accelerated through [[Muddler|muddling]] or [[Abrasion (mechanical)|mechanical abrasion]] of the mixture, which helps to rupture oil-rich [[vacuole]]s on the rinds' surface or [[flavedo]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Oleo-Saccharum|url=https://libationlaboratory.com/tag/oleo-saccharum/|access-date=2020-08-20|website=Libation Laboratory|language=en}}</ref>

A frequent misconception is that oil extraction occurs due to sugar's [[Hygroscopy|hygroscopic]] nature, though this is unlikely as the essential oils being extracted are hydrophobic. A similar misconception is that the extraction occurs via [[osmosis]]; the sugar cannot dissolve in the oil, so there is no sugar-solute based osmotic [[gradient]].{{Cn|date=May 2025}}

