==Serving Lillet==
Lillet is an aperitif wine, intended to be served well chilled at 6–8&nbsp;°C (43–46&nbsp;°F). In France, Lillet is usually served on ice with a slice of orange, or a lemon or lime peel. In Germany, Austria and Switzerland Lillet blanc is more often served as a Lillet Vive, a Lillet blanc [[long drink]]. A Lillet Vive is 5cl Lillet blanc, 10cl tonic water, a slice of cucumber, a strawberry and mint leaves.

In other countries, especially in the US and UK, it is more often used as a cocktail ingredient. The best known Lillet cocktails are the [[Vesper (cocktail)|Vesper]], the [[Corpse Reviver#Corpse Reviver №2 & №2A|Corpse Reviver #2]], the [[20th Century (cocktail)|20th Century]] and the [[Old Etonian (cocktail)|Old Etonian]]. Recipes appear in cocktail books including ''The Bartender's Bible'' by Gary Regan, the ''Savoy Cocktail Book'' by [[Harry Craddock]] and the ''Complete World Bartender Guide'' by Bob Sennett. In the 1930s the ''Savoy Cocktail Book'' published 22 Lillet cocktails recipes (46 Cointreau, 24 Dubonnet, 24 Chartreuse and 10 Grand Marnier, by comparison).<ref name="londeix"/>{{rp|p=147}}

A Lillet spritz is a two-ingredient cocktail in which white or rosé Lillet is mixed with [[sparkling water]] and a garnish such as mint, berries, cranberries, or thyme sprigs.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://vinepair.com/articles/20-best-two-ingredient-cocktail-drinks-recipes/|title = 20 of the Best Two-Ingredient Cocktails|date = 19 December 2018}}</ref>  In the United States, a booklet titled "The Lillet Book of Apertif Cocktails" was distributed with bottles of Lillet.  It included a recipe which described a "Lillet Spritz" as "3 parts Lillet Blanc or Lillet Rose and 3 parts tonic water.  Build over 3 large ice cubes in a tall wine glass and garnish with cucumber, mint and strawberry."

== Appearances in popular culture ==
* 1950s: [[Wallis Simpson|Wallis, Duchess of Windsor]], the American spouse of King Edward VIII, was a great admirer of Lillet. She introduced it  to high society, particularly at Fauchon, and into the upper-class hotels where she stayed frequently.{{fact|date=June 2021}}
* 1953: in the first [[Ian Fleming]] [[James Bond]] novel ''[[Casino Royale (novel)|Casino Royale]]'', Bond invents a Kina Lillet [[Martini (cocktail)|martini]], which he named the "[[Vesper (cocktail)|Vesper]]" after [[Vesper Lynd|his love interest]] in the story:  
:{{Quote|"A dry martini," he said. "One. In a deep champagne goblet." "Oui, Monsieur." "Just a moment. Three measures of Gordon's, one of vodka, half a measure of Kina Lillet. Shake it very well until it's ice-cold, then add a large thin slice of lemon-peel. Got it?" "Certainly, monsieur." The barman seemed pleased with the idea.}}
:The Vesper was prepared for James Bond in the films ''[[Casino Royale (2006 film)|Casino Royale]]'' (2006) and ''[[Quantum of Solace]]'' (2008).<ref>{{Citation|last=Today I Found Out|title=Why Does James Bond Like His Martinis Shaken Not Stirred?|date=2017-01-16|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5t3U7QlSNlQ|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211219/5t3U7QlSNlQ|access-date=2017-01-23|archive-date=2021-12-19|url-status=live}}{{cbignore}}</ref>
*1981: Lillet is the preferred drink of serial killer [[Hannibal Lecter]] in the series of books written by [[Thomas Harris]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.chicagomag.com/Chicago-Magazine/July-2012/Its-Cocktail-Hour-for-Lillet/|title=It's Cocktail Hour for Lillet|website=Chicago Magazine}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/theguardian/2001/apr/28/weekend7.weekend6|title=Down in one|first=Fiona|last=Beckett|date=April 28, 2001|newspaper=The Guardian}}</ref>
*2018: Lillet is featured in [[Dan Fesperman]] novel ''Safe Houses''.
*2022: Lillet is featured in the German-language track "Wildberry Lillet" by [[Nina Chuba]].

