Peychaud's Bitters:
{{short description|Gentian-based bitters}}
{{Use American English|date=April 2026}}
{{Use mdy dates|date=April 2026}}
[[File:Peychauds.jpg|right|thumb|180 px|Peychaud's Bitters]]
'''Peychaud's bitters''' is a [[bitters]] distributed by the American [[Sazerac Company]].<ref>{{cite web |url = https://www.sazerac.com/our-brands/sazerac-brands/peychauds.html | archiveurl = https://web.archive.org/web/20080118004337/http://www.sazerac.com/bitters.html | publisher = The Sazerac Company | location = New Orleans, LA | title = Peychaud's Bitters | url-status = live | archivedate = 2008-01-18 | accessdate = 2020-10-17}}</ref><ref name = SazeracWeb>{{cite web | url = http://www.sazerac.com/company.aspx | title = The Sazerac Company Web Site | accessdate = 2013-08-04}}</ref> It was created between 1849 and 1857 by Antoine Amédée Peychaud, a [[Creole peoples|Creole]] [[apothecary]] from the French colony of [[Saint-Domingue]] (now [[Haiti]]) who traveled to [[New Orleans, Louisiana]], around 1793.<ref>{{cite OCSC|pages=534-535}}</ref> It is a [[Gentiana|gentian]]-based bitters, comparable to [[Angostura bitters]], but with a predominant [[anise]] aroma combined with a background of mint.<ref name = "Trib">{{Cite news | first = M. Carrie | last = Allan | title = Bitters are essential to a good cocktail, but which ones should you buy? | newspaper = [[Chicago Tribune]] | location = Chicago, Illinois  |  date = 7 February 2018 | url = http://www.chicagotribune.com/dining/drink/ct-bitters-for-cocktails-20180207-story.html |url-status=live | archiveurl = https://web.archive.org/web/20180207223901/http://www.chicagotribune.com/dining/drink/ct-bitters-for-cocktails-20180207-story.html | archivedate = 7 February 2018 | accessdate = 17 May 2018 }}</ref> It is currently produced at the [[Buffalo Trace Distillery]] in Frankfort, Kentucky.

==Cocktails==
{{Category see also|Cocktails with Peychaud's bitters}}
Peychaud's bitters is a distinctive ingredient in certain cocktails, particularly those from New Orleans, most famously the [[Sazerac (cocktail)|Sazerac cocktail]]<ref name = NPR>{{Cite news |url=https://www.npr.org/templates/story/story.php?storyId=91912549 |title= New Orleans Declares Sazerac Its Cocktail of Choice |publisher=National Public Radio |journal=All Things Considered |date=June 26, 2008 | accessdate = 2013-08-04}}</ref><ref name = SazeracRecipe>{{Cite web |url=http://www.sazerac.com/cocktail.aspx | title= The Sazerac — Official Cocktail of New Orleans: How the Sazerac Cocktail Came to Be | accessdate = 2013-08-04}}</ref> and the [[Vieux Carré (cocktail)|Vieux Carré]]. Other cocktails that feature Peychaud's bitters include the [[Pendennis Club (cocktail)|Pendennis Club]].

