'''''The World's Drinks And How To Mix Them''''' is a [[cocktail]] manual by [[William "Cocktail" Boothby]] originally published in 1891, with revised editions in 1908, 1930 and 1934. The publisher was the [[Palace Hotel, San Francisco|Palace Hotel]] in San Francisco, where Boothby worked.

The cover of the 1908 edition describes this as the "Standard Authority" by a "Premier Mixologist".

The book published the first recipe for the [[sazerac]],<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.cocktailhall.org/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=31&Itemid=36 |title=The Sazerac |date=April 2011 |publisher=The Institute for Cocktail Excellence }}</ref> one of the earliest recipes for a [[dry martini]],<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=h5igQMFsCtUC&dq=The+World%27s+Drinks+And+How+To+Mix+Them&pg=PA64|title=Miss Charming's Guide for Hip Bartenders and Wayout Wannabes: Your Ultimate One-Stop Bar and Cocktail Resource|first=Cheryl|last=Charming|date=October 1, 2006|publisher=Sourcebooks, Inc.|isbn=9781402250040|via=Google Books}}</ref> as well as the [[Bronx (cocktail)|Bronx cocktail]].<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=teFJBgAAQBAJ&dq=%22The+World%27s+Drinks+And+How+To+Mix+Them%22&pg=PT46|title=To Have and Have Another Revised Edition: A Hemingway Cocktail Companion|first=Philip|last=Greene|date=November 3, 2015|publisher=Penguin|isbn=9780698407169|via=Google Books}}</ref>

