Downtown Hotel:
{{Short description|Building in Yukon, Canada}}
{{Use Canadian English|date=January 2026}}
[[File:Panorama of Downtown Hotel - Dawson City - Yukon Territory - Canada.jpg|thumb|right|Downtown Hotel in 2013]]

The '''Downtown Hotel''' is an establishment at Second Avenue and Queen Street in [[Dawson City]], [[Yukon]], Canada.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Burgess |first1=Ann Carroll |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=TuggI-Ye_AUC&pg=PA281 |title=Guide to Western Canada |last2=Burgess |first2=Tom |publisher=Globe Pequot Press |year=2005 |isbn=978-0-7627-2987-6 |page=281}}{{Dead link|date=September 2023|bot=InternetArchiveBot|fix-attempted=yes}}</ref> It contains 59 rooms.<ref>{{cite web |title=Rooms |url=http://downtownhotel.ca/rooms/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140908044654/http://downtownhotel.ca/rooms/ |archive-date=8 September 2014 |access-date=7 September 2014 |website=Downtown Hotel}}</ref>

==History==
Contrary to popular belief, the current hotel structure was not built in the [[Klondike Gold Rush|late 1890s Gold Rush]] because great fires, storms and flooding have weathered away any previous buildings. The current structure was built in [[gold rush|Gold Rush]] [[boomtown]] style in the early 1980s, conforming to modern city building codes and catering to modern tourism.<ref> {{cite web |date=March 2008 |title=Dawson City Heritage Management Plan |url=https://www.cityofdawson.ca/Home/DownloadDocument?docId=8f49bb2e-5bf2-4641-8562-511fa6dceb0a |access-date=9 July 2023 |publisher=City of Dawson}}</ref>

==Sourtoe cocktail==
[[File:Sourtoe Cocktail Preparation - Downtown Hotel, Dawson City, Yukon Territory (12448567845).jpg|thumb|right|The mummified toe used in the drink|alt=A blackened mummified toe on a pile of salt]]
The hotel has gained notoriety for its Sourtoe cocktail. The Sourtoe cocktail began during [[Prohibition in Canada|Prohibition]] with a case of [[frostbite]]. In the 1920s, two outlaw brothers, Louie and Otto, were caught in a blizzard. Louie soaked his foot, and when the brothers got back to their cabin, Louie's foot was frostbitten with his right toe becoming gangrenous. Otto amputated it, and placed it in a jar filled with [[Bourbon whiskey|bourbon]] to commemorate the event.<ref>{{cite web |title=The history of the Sourtoe Cocktail: a shot of whiskey garnished with a human toe |url=https://www.cbc.ca/shortdocs/blog/the-story-of-the-sourtoe-cocktail-a-shot-of-whiskey-garnished-with-a-human |access-date=18 July 2019 |website=[[Canadian Broadcasting Corporation|CBC]]}}</ref> The drink tradition was established by riverboat captain Dick Stevenson in 1973.<ref>{{cite web |date=14 November 2019 |title=Captain Dick Stevenson, inventor of Yukon's infamous 'Sourtoe Cocktail,' has died |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/north/captain-dick-stevenson-sourtoe-obituary-1.5359630 |website=[[CBC News]]}} </ref>

To gain admittance to a club of drinkers of the Sourtoe cocktail, members must drink the cocktail and the lips of the participant must touch the toe. Participants are presented with a signed certificate; however, ingesting the toe results in a $2,500 fine and permanent barring from the premises, up from $500 from when a guest deliberately ingested it in 2013, proactively paying the fine and unremorsefully shocking the bartender.<ref>{{cite web |last=Ballingall |first=Alex |date=27 August 2013 |title=Yukon bar patron swallows famous sourtoe, pays fine, leaves town |url=https://www.thestar.com/news/canada/2013/08/27/yukon_bar_patron_swallows_famous_sourtoe_pays_fine_leaves_town.html |access-date=9 July 2023 |website=[[Toronto Star]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Dawson City and the Sourtoe Cocktail |url=https://calibremag.com/dawson-city-and-the-sourtoe-cocktail/ |website=Calibre}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Sourdough Saloon |url=https://downtownhotel.ca/sourdough-saloon/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140908135301/http://downtownhotel.ca/sourdough-saloon/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=September 8, 2014 |website=Downtown Hotel}}</ref> Over 100,000 customers have tried the concoction to date, as of 2023. Bartender Terry Lee says they would like to have another toe donated, because several toes have been damaged, stolen, swallowed or lost over the span of decades.<ref>{{cite web |date=27 August 2013 |title=Customer swallows human toe in Dawson City bar |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/north/customer-swallows-human-toe-in-dawson-city-bar-1.1331325 |access-date=18 July 2019 |website=[[Canadian Broadcasting Corporation|CBC]]}}</ref>

