Zurracapote:
{{Short description|Alcoholic beverage}}
{{Refimprove|date=June 2007}}
{{Infobox drink
| name        = Zurracapote
| image       = Preparando Zurracapote.jpg  
| caption     = Preparing Zurracapote 
| type        = [[Wine cocktail]]
| base        = 
| ingredients ={{plainlist|* [[red wine]]
* fruit ([[peach]]es, [[lemon]]s)
* [[sugar]]
* [[cinnamon]]}}
| drinkware   = [[Goblet]]
}}

'''Zurracapote''' (sometimes abbreviated as '''zurra''') is a popular [[cuisine of Spain|Spanish]] [[alcohol (drug)|alcohol]]ic [[cocktail|mixed drink]], similar to [[sangría]]. It consists of [[red wine]] mixed with fruit such as peaches and lemons, [[sugar]], and [[cinnamon]]. The concoction is then traditionally left to steep for several days, though some recipes call for the addition of other alcoholic beverages, juices, and fruit extracts. The result is a mild-to-medium alcoholic drink, similar to [[sangría]].

The drink is normally prepared in large receptacles during local festivals with many local variations in recipe. It was first drunk in Calahorra, where "Peña Phillips" developed the recipe through several competitions. It is the typical drink of the [[La Rioja (Spain)|La Rioja]] area and nearby areas such as northern [[Burgos]], [[Soria]], [[Ribera Navarra]], and the [[Basque Country (autonomous community)|Basque Country]]. It is also prepared in other Spanish provinces, such as [[Albacete (province)|Albacete]], northern [[Granada (province)|Granada]], [[Ciudad Real]], [[Province of Cuenca|Cuenca]], and [[Guadalajara (province)|Guadalajara]].

